"titre": "Can We Be Happy by Living with Less?",
"titre2": "Exercice b005 (Personal Essay / Récit réflexif)",
"texte": [
"Three years ago, my apartment resembled a storage unit bursting at the seams. Every closet was crammed with unworn clothes, outdated gadgets, and impulse purchases that had long lost their appeal. Although I earned a comfortable salary, I constantly felt anxious—both financially and mentally. It was only when a sudden flood forced me to pack my essentials into a single suitcase for two months that I experienced an unexpected epiphany: I missed almost nothing I had left behind.",
"That incident sparked my transition toward intentional minimalism. Initially, skeptics among my peers assumed I was adopting a life of grim deprivation. On the contrary, shedding physical excess felt profoundly liberating. Whereas owning numerous possessions had once required endless maintenance, cleaning, and organizing, living with less restored my most precious non-renewable resource: time. Furthermore, my monthly expenses plummeted, allowing me to reduce my working hours without jeopardizing my security.",
"Of course, minimalism is not a universal panacea, nor should it be confused with involuntary poverty. Choosing simplicity from a position of privilege differs vastly from lacking basic necessities. Nevertheless, for those trapped in the cycle of material accumulation, voluntary simplicity offers a compelling alternative. Ultimately, true wealth is rarely measured by the abundance of what we own, but rather by the scarcity of what we actually need to feel content."
],
