"titre2": "Exercice b015 (Argumentative Essay / Essai)",
"texte": [
"Education is widely celebrated as the ultimate social elevator—an impartial institution capable of rewarding talent and diligence regardless of a child’s birthplace. In theory, every student enters the classroom with an equal opportunity to flourish. In practice, however, persistent educational inequalities frequently transform schools from engines of social mobility into mechanisms that reproduce existing class divides. When access to quality instruction depends heavily on postal codes and household income, the consequences extend far beyond individual report cards, threatening the long-term cohesion and prosperity of entire societies.",
"The roots of educational disparity are multifaceted and manifest long before a child steps into a primary classroom. Children raised in affluent households typically benefit from enriched early-childhood environments, private tutoring, stable internet access, and extracurricular enrichment. Conversely, underfunded schools in marginalized neighborhoods often grapple with overcrowded classrooms, outdated textbooks, and chronic teacher shortages. Consequently, disadvantaged students must exert exponentially greater effort merely to reach the starting line that their wealthier peers take for granted.",
"Furthermore, the repercussions of these unequal foundations compound dramatically over a lifetime. Lower academic attainment restricts access to prestigious universities and high-growth professions, trapping successive generations in cycles of precarious employment and lower life expectancy. On a macroeconomic level, failing to nurture the intellectual potential of low-income youth represents an immense waste of human capital. Nations deprive themselves of future innovators, scientists, and leaders simply because those individuals lacked adequate resources during their formative years.",
"Nevertheless, educational inequality is neither inevitable nor irreversible. Countries that have successfully narrowed achievement gaps demonstrate that targeted public investment—such as universal early-childhood programs, equitable school funding formulas, and incentives to attract experienced educators to underserved areas—yields profound social returns. Ultimately, ensuring genuine equality of educational opportunity is not merely a moral obligation toward disadvantaged children; it is a pragmatic investment in a fairer, more innovative, and more stable collective future."
