    "titre": "Should Student Assessment Methods Be Reformed?",
    "titre2": "Exercice b016 (Speech / Discours)",
    "texte": [
      "Esteemed colleagues, policymakers, and student representatives,",
      "For more than a century, standardized examinations and numerical grades have served as the undisputed currency of academic achievement. Every spring, millions of adolescents sit in silent gymnasiums, racing against the clock to demonstrate years of intellectual growth within a rigid three-hour window. Nevertheless, as our understanding of human cognition and workplace demands evolves, we must confront an uncomfortable truth: our traditional assessment methods frequently measure compliance and short-term memorization rather than genuine comprehension or intellectual curiosity.",
      "On the one hand, defenders of conventional testing rightly emphasize the importance of objectivity and comparability. Standardized exams provide universities and employers with a uniform benchmark to evaluate candidates from vastly different regions and socioeconomic backgrounds. Without clear, quantifiable criteria, admissions processes could easily succumb to subjective bias or personal favoritism. In other words, grades offer a convenient, transparent shorthand for sorting large populations efficiently.",
      "On the other hand, relying exclusively on high-stakes examinations produces severe unintended consequences. When a student’s entire academic future hinges on a single test score, anxiety skyrockets, and learning degenerates into strategic cramming. Once the exam concludes, much of the memorized material evaporates within weeks. Furthermore, traditional paper-and-pencil assessments struggle to capture vital twenty-first-century competencies—such as collaborative problem-solving, oral persuasion, resilience in the face of ambiguity, and creative synthesis. Consequently, many exceptionally capable learners who struggle under artificial time constraints are unfairly labeled as underachievers.",
      "Reforming assessment does not mean abandoning academic rigor; rather, it requires broadening our evaluative toolkit. Progressive education systems are already demonstrating the value of portfolio assessments, project-based evaluations, and continuous formative feedback that guides students while they are still learning, rather than merely judging them at the finish line. Ultimately, if we want schools to cultivate deep thinkers instead of anxious test-takers, we must redesign our exams to reflect the qualities we genuinely value. Thank you."
